<p class="lh">Many teams treat website launch as the finish line. In practice, launch is when operational responsibility begins. Without a maintenance plan, security patches slip, performance degrades, backups go untested, and small issues compound into costly outages or breaches.</p>

<h2 id="why-maintenance">Why maintenance starts on day one</h2>
<p class="lh">Frameworks, plugins, server runtimes, and browser standards change continuously. A site that passed QA at launch can develop vulnerabilities within weeks if dependencies are not monitored. Search engines also reward fresh, fast, stable sites — maintenance directly supports SEO and conversion performance.</p>

<h2 id="security">Security and patch management</h2>
<p class="lh">Establish a monthly patch rhythm for CMS cores, plugins, and server packages. Review admin account access, enforce multi-factor authentication, and scan for exposed credentials or misconfigured storage. After critical CVE announcements, stage patches quickly and regression-test checkout, forms, and login flows before production deployment.</p>
<p class="lh">Our <a href="website-maintenance.php">website maintenance</a> and <a href="security-auditing.php">security auditing</a> services include dependency monitoring, penetration testing cadence, and incident response runbooks for custom Laravel, Node.js, and WordPress stacks.</p>

<h2 id="performance">Performance and uptime</h2>
<p class="lh">Monitor Core Web Vitals, API latency, and error rates weekly. Set alerts for downtime and 5xx spikes. Review slow database queries and CDN cache hit rates quarterly. Load-test before major campaigns or product launches — traffic spikes expose bottlenecks that steady-state monitoring misses.</p>

<h2 id="content">Content and CMS operations</h2>
<p class="lh">Train content editors on publishing workflows, image optimization, and accessibility basics (alt text, heading hierarchy, link labels). Archive outdated pages instead of leaving broken links. Backup CMS databases and media libraries on a schedule you have actually restored from — an untested backup is not a backup.</p>

<h2 id="conclusion">The bottom line</h2>
<p class="lh">Website maintenance is not optional overhead — it protects the investment you made in custom development. Pair launch with a clear ownership model, SLA targets, and a partner who can handle patches, performance, and enhancements as your product evolves. <a href="contact-us.php">Contact SanguineIT</a> to discuss a maintenance plan for your site.</p>
